   {"slug":"excavation-not-demolition","title":"Deconstructing Your Faith: What to Tear Down and What to Keep","excerpt":"Much of what people tear down when they deconstruct was never the Christian faith, and the work is learning to tell the difference.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"is-it-wrong-to-love-your-country","title":"Is It Wrong to Love Your Country?","excerpt":"Scripture treats love of country as a real good, and the kind of love it honors grieves a country's sins and names them.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"the-children-rome-threw-away","title":"What the First Christians Did About the Children Rome Threw Away","excerpt":"When leaving an unwanted newborn outside was an ordinary Roman family decision, the first Christians refused to do it, and the history of that refusal still bears on how the church treats unwanted children and their mothers.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
+  {"slug":"the-council-of-nicaea-what-was-decided-in-325","title":"What Was Decided at the Council of Nicaea?","excerpt":"In 325 the bishops at Nicaea decided that Jesus Christ is not the first and greatest of God's creatures but God in the same full sense as the Father, and most of the world's churches still confess what they wrote.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"the-early-church-under-rome","title":"When the Church Had Nothing to Lose","excerpt":"For almost three centuries the church lived under an empire that could kill it whenever it chose, and how it lived and grew there is history a post-Christian America needs to learn.","updated":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"the-historical-jesus","title":"Was Jesus Real? What Historians Actually Know","excerpt":"Nearly every historian of the ancient world, believer or not, agrees that Jesus of Nazareth lived and was crucified under Pontius Pilate, and the evidence says more about him than most people expect.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
+  {"slug":"the-persecution-we-invented","title":"Is the American Church Really Being Persecuted?","excerpt":"Most of what American Christians now call persecution is the loss of a privileged place in the culture, and the New Testament teaches a different way to bear that loss than the one we have learned.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"the-rise-of-the-nones","title":"Why Are People Leaving the Church?","excerpt":"Millions of Americans have left the church since the 1990s, and many of them simply drifted away from a faith the church had made too thin to hold them.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"was-america-founded-as-a-christian-nation","title":"Was America Founded as a Christian Nation?","excerpt":"A deeply Protestant people built a national government that declined to call itself Christian, and some of the Christians who fought hardest to keep church and state apart were Baptists.","updated":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},
+  {"slug":"what-a-christian-owes-the-city","title":"What Does It Mean to Seek the Welfare of the City?","excerpt":"Jeremiah's command to seek the welfare of Babylon tells Christians what they owe the places where they live, and the church has had to learn it again from Carthage to Birmingham.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"what-christian-nationalism-is-and-is-not","title":"What Christian Nationalism Is, and What It Is Not","excerpt":"Christian nationalism is a fusion of Christian faith and national belonging, and it is a different thing from the love of country that Scripture assumes.","updated":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"what-post-christian-means","title":"What \"Post-Christian\" Actually Means","excerpt":"What has ended in America is Christendom, the old arrangement in which the culture handed people the faith, and Jeremiah's letter to the exiles shows the church how to live after it.","updated":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},
+  {"slug":"what-render-unto-caesar-actually-means","title":"What Did Jesus Mean by \"Render Unto Caesar\"?","excerpt":"Jesus said \"Render to Caesar\" in the temple during the last week of his life, when men who wanted him dead asked him whether to pay a Roman tax.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"what-romans-13-says","title":"What Does Romans 13 Say About Obeying the Government?","excerpt":"Romans 13 tells Christians to be subject to whoever governs, and, read in its place in Paul's letter, it also tells them what government is for.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
+  {"slug":"what-the-original-audience-heard","title":"What the First Readers of the Bible Actually Heard","excerpt":"Every book of the Bible was first heard aloud by particular people in a particular place, and reading it well begins with hearing what they heard.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"who-decided-what-books-are-in-the-bible","title":"Who Decided What Books Are in the Bible?","excerpt":"The New Testament was gathered over about three centuries as the churches read its books aloud in worship, and the letters and lists that show how it happened can still be read today.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"why-didnt-the-bible-ban-slavery","title":"Why Didn't the Bible Just Ban Slavery?","excerpt":"The Bible never bans slavery outright, and the American Christians who defended slavery explained away the texts in that same Bible that condemned what they were doing.","updated":"2026-10-27T00:00:00.000Z"},
   {"slug":"why-does-god-allow-suffering","title":"Why Does God Allow Suffering?","excerpt":"Suffering is the hardest objection to the Christian faith, and the Bible faces it more squarely than many of its defenders do.","updated":"2026-10-28T00:00:00.000Z"},
